  "# Codegen co-scientist, iteration 3

## The criterion under test

Iteration 2 concluded that instruction count is a bad proxy for time and
proposed a better one: **rank by whether the candidate shortens the dependency
chain.** D1 was generated under that criterion. The division computes in R11
and moves its answer to the allocator's register; the next instruction cannot
start until that move retires; the folded three-operand forms can read R11
directly, so the move can go.

## Measure

498 → 474 bytes, eight moves removed, three bytes each. 25 rotated samples
under Rosetta: **25.09 median against 24.95 without it.** Inside this host's
drift, and if anything the wrong way.

## The criterion is also wrong

A register-to-register move on this class of core is **renamed, not executed**.
It occupies no port and adds no cycle. It is on the dependency chain and it is
free. The criterion moved from counting instructions to locating them, and both
versions were measuring the wrong thing, because both assumed every instruction
costs something.

Three passes now have removed real instructions and changed no time:

| iteration | removed | time |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | a register copy | none |
| 2 | eight off-chain constant loads | none |
| 3 | a register copy on the chain | none |

And what did move it, earlier the same day:

| change | removed | time |
|---|---|---|
| immediate folding | a uop — `mov` plus an operation became one operation | moved |
| `lea` fusion | two operations became one | moved |
| stack-free division | **memory round trips** | moved |

**The corrected model: this core charges for memory traffic and for real
operations, and does not charge for moving a value between registers.** Every
result in both tables fits it.

## Meta-review

- **A fitness proxy has to model the machine, not the listing.** Three
  successive proxies — count, then position, then position again — all assumed
  instructions cost uniformly. The machine has said otherwise three times.
- **The measurable distance to LLVM has not moved since immediate folding.**
  x86-64 output went 896 bytes to 474 today, a 47% reduction, on a function
  whose measured time has been roughly 1.2x rustc throughout. Bytes and time
  came apart at that point and every candidate since has been buying bytes.
- **Therefore: stop adding peepholes to this workload.** Not because they are
  wrong — all three are correct, tested and landed for their bytes — but
  because the remaining distance is not in the instruction stream they edit.
  A new peephole here should now have to name the memory traffic or the real
  operations it removes before it is ranked at all.
- **The generator is exhausted before the domain is.** Every candidate in three
  iterations was read off one disassembly of one kernel. A kernel with
  allocation, calls, or a working set larger than the register file would
  produce a different list, and this one has said everything it has to say.

## Next

Two directions, neither a peephole.

Allocation: 39 register-to-register moves remain, and by the corrected model
most of them are free, so the win there is not the moves themselves but the
spills and reloads a four-register allocator forces on anything larger than
this kernel. That is memory traffic, which is what this core charges for.

A second workload. The measurement has been one kernel all day. Its shape —
scalar, branchless, register-resident — is exactly the shape that hides
allocation costs."}]
